# .spool/config/config.req

feature: CONFIG

requirements:
  - id: CONFIG-1
    title: Config File Loading
    description: >
      Spool must load its configuration from a .spool.yaml
      file at the project root.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-03-23
    rationale: >
      A single config file at the project root is the most
      discoverable convention and consistent with the broader
      ecosystem of developer tooling. The .spool namespace
      keeps all Spool artifacts clearly identifiable.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: CONFIG-1-1
        title: Valid Config Parsing
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml file at the project root,
          when config is loaded,
          then the file is parsed and a populated Config
          struct is returned.

      - id: CONFIG-1-2
        title: Missing Config Defaults
        description: >
          Given no .spool.yaml file at the project root,
          when config is loaded,
          then a Config struct populated with default values
          is returned and no error is produced.

      - id: CONFIG-1-3
        title: Invalid YAML Error
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml file with invalid YAML,
          when config is loaded,
          then a descriptive error is returned.

      - id: CONFIG-1-4
        title: Unknown Fields Ignored
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml file with valid YAML but unknown
          fields,
          when config is loaded,
          then the known fields are populated, unknown fields
          are ignored, and no error is produced.

  - id: CONFIG-2
    title: Reqs Directory Configuration
    description: >
      Spool must allow the reqs directory to be configured
      and must default to .spool/ relative to the project root.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-03-23
    rationale: >
      A configurable reqs directory allows institutions to place
      their .req files wherever suits their project structure.
      The .spool/ default keeps Spool's footprint namespaced and
      tidy without requiring any configuration for new projects.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:
      - id: CONFIG-2-1
        title: Configured Reqs Directory
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml with a reqs_dir value,
          when config is loaded,
          then the ReqsDir field reflects the configured value.

      - id: CONFIG-2-2
        title: Default Reqs Directory
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml with no reqs_dir value,
          when config is loaded,
          then the ReqsDir field defaults to .spool/.

      - id: CONFIG-2-3
        title: Relative Path Resolution
        description: >
          Given a reqs_dir configured as a relative path,
          when config is loaded,
          then the path is resolved relative to the project root.

  - id: CONFIG-3
    title: Test Pattern Configuration
    description: >
      Spool must allow one or more glob patterns to be configured
      identifying which files the scanner should treat as test files.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-03-23
    rationale: >
      Glob-based test file identification is simple, universal,
      and sufficient for the vast majority of projects. A smart
      test file detector may be introduced later if demand warrants
      it, but configurable patterns avoid premature complexity
      while keeping Spool immediately useful across different
      languages and project structures.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: CONFIG-3-1
        title: Configured Test Patterns
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml with a test_patterns list,
          when config is loaded,
          then the TestPatterns field reflects all configured patterns.

      - id: CONFIG-3-2
        title: Default Test Patterns
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml with no test_patterns value,
          when config is loaded,
          then the TestPatterns field defaults to an empty slice
          and no error is produced.

      - id: CONFIG-3-3
        title: Invalid Glob Error
        description: >
          Given a test_patterns list containing an invalid glob,
          when config is loaded,
          then a descriptive error is returned identifying
          the invalid pattern.

  - id: CONFIG-4
    title: Diagnostic Severity Configuration
    description: >
      Spool must allow the severity of each diagnostic class
      to be configured independently.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-11-03
    rationale: >
      Different institutions have different tolerances for
      diagnostic severity, so each class stays configurable.
      The defaults, though, state Spool's position: an untested
      AC is a failure, because tracing every AC to a test is the
      point of the tool. A project that tolerates untested ACs
      must say so in its .spool.yaml. Until 2026-11-03 the
      default for untested_ac was warning.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: CONFIG-4-1
        title: Configured Severity Values
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml with severity values configured,
          when config is loaded,
          then the Severity struct reflects all configured values.

      - id: CONFIG-4-2
        title: Default Severity Values
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml with no severity values configured,
          when config is loaded,
          then the following defaults are applied:
          untested_ac as error, orphaned_annotation as error,
          and missing_ac as info.

      - id: CONFIG-4-3
        title: Invalid Severity Error
        description: >
          Given a severity value that is not one of error,
          warning, info, or off,
          when config is loaded,
          then a descriptive error is returned identifying
          the invalid value.
  - id: CONFIG-5
    title: Glob Pattern Matching
    description: >
      Spool must match test_patterns and exclude_patterns against
      each path relative to the project root, with ** matching
      any number of directories.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-10-30
    rationale: >
      The original matcher discarded everything before a **, so
      tests/**/*.rs matched every .rs file in the project and a
      pattern could not be limited to a directory tree. Matching
      the whole relative path, segment by segment, makes the
      prefix meaningful while keeping existing patterns such as
      **/*_test.go working unchanged.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: CONFIG-5-1
        title: Plain Pattern Matches Whole Path
        description: >
          Given the pattern tests/*.rs,
          when paths are matched,
          then tests/a.rs matches and tests/sub/a.rs and
          src/a.rs do not.

      - id: CONFIG-5-2
        title: Prefix Before Double Star Respected
        description: >
          Given the pattern tests/**/*.rs,
          when paths are matched,
          then tests/a.rs and tests/sub/deep/a.rs match
          and src/a.rs and src/tests/a.rs do not.

      - id: CONFIG-5-3
        title: Leading Double Star Matches Any Depth
        description: >
          Given the pattern **/*_test.go,
          when paths are matched,
          then a_test.go and pkg/sub/a_test.go match
          and pkg/a.go does not.

      - id: CONFIG-5-4
        title: Multiple Double Stars
        description: >
          Given the pattern **/tests/**/*.rs,
          when paths are matched,
          then tests/a.rs and crates/x/tests/sub/a.rs match
          and crates/x/src/a.rs does not.

  - id: CONFIG-6
    title: Exclude Pattern Configuration
    description: >
      Spool must allow glob patterns to be configured identifying
      directories and files the scanner should never read.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-10-30
    rationale: >
      Build output and dependency directories (target, dist,
      node_modules, vendor) differ by language, so a hard-coded
      skip list grows with every language Spool supports.
      exclude_patterns moves that list into the project. The
      defaults reproduce the previously hard-coded skips so
      existing projects are unaffected, and setting the key
      replaces the defaults so a project controls the full list.
      .git is always skipped because it is never source.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: CONFIG-6-1
        title: Configured Exclude Patterns
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml with an exclude_patterns list,
          when config is loaded,
          then the ExcludePatterns field reflects exactly the
          configured patterns.

      - id: CONFIG-6-2
        title: Default Exclude Patterns
        description: >
          Given a .spool.yaml with no exclude_patterns value,
          when config is loaded,
          then the ExcludePatterns field is **/node_modules
          and **/vendor and no error is produced.

      - id: CONFIG-6-3
        title: Invalid Exclude Glob Error
        description: >
          Given an exclude_patterns list containing an invalid glob,
          when config is loaded,
          then a descriptive error is returned identifying
          the invalid pattern.

      - id: CONFIG-6-4
        title: Excluded Directory Not Walked
        description: >
          Given exclude_patterns containing target and a target
          directory holding files that match test_patterns,
          when test files are discovered,
          then no file under target is returned.

      - id: CONFIG-6-5
        title: Excluded File Not Scanned
        description: >
          Given a file that matches both test_patterns and
          exclude_patterns,
          when test files are discovered,
          then the file is not returned.

      - id: CONFIG-6-6
        title: Git Directory Always Skipped
        description: >
          Given exclude_patterns that do not mention .git,
          when test files are discovered,
          then no file under .git is returned.
